== Chapter XII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[#emph[The Eighth Direction. Thoughtfulness of the Excellency of the Majesty of God. Our Unacquaintedness with him, proposed and considered.]]
]

==== Eighth Direction: Meditations That Fill the Soul with Self-Abasement

#emph[#smallcaps[Eighthly],] Use and exercise thyself to such #emph[Meditations] as may serve to fill thee at all times with #emph[self-abasement] and Thoughts of thine own #emph[vileness:] As,

1\. Be much in Thoughtfulness of the #emph[Excellency] of the Majesty of God, and thine #emph[infinite inconceivable distance] from him; many thoughts of it cannot but #emph[fill] thee with a sense of thine own Vileness, which strikes deep at the #emph[root] of any indwelling Sin. When #emph[Job] comes to a #emph[clear] discovery of the Greatness and #emph[Excellency] of God, he is filled with #emph[self-abhorrency,] and is pressed to Humiliation, Job 42:5, 6. And in what #emph[state] doth the #emph[Prophet Habakkuk] affirm himself to be cast, upon the apprehension of the #emph[Majesty] of God? ch. 3:16. #emph[With God] (says #emph[Job]) #emph[is terrible Majesty,] Job 37:22. Hence were the thoughts of them of Old, that when they had seen #emph[God] they should die. The Scripture abounds in this #emph[self-abasing Consideration,] comparing the men of the Earth to #emph[Grasshoppers,] to #emph[Vanity,] the #emph[dust] of the #emph[balance] in respect of God, Isa 40:13, 14, 15. Be much in thoughts of this #emph[nature,] to abase the pride of thy Heart, and to keep thy Soul #emph[humble] within thee. There is nothing will render thee in a greater #emph[indisposition] to be imposed on by the deceits of sin, than such a frame of Heart. Think #emph[greatly] of the #emph[Greatness] of God.

2\. Think much of thine #emph[unacquaintedness] with him; Though thou knowest #emph[enough] to keep thee #emph[low] and #emph[humble,] yet how little #emph[a portion] is it that thou knowest of him! The Contemplation hereof cast that wise man into that #emph[Apprehension] of himself, which he expresses, Prov 30:2, 3, 4. #emph[Surely I am more brutish than any man, and have not the understanding of a man. I neither learned Wisdom, nor have the knowledge of the Holy. Who hath ascended up into Heaven, or descended? Who hath gathered the Wind in his fists? Who hath bound the Waters in a Garment? Who hath established the Ends of the Earth? What is his Name, and what is his Sons Name if thou canst tell?] Labor with this also to take down the pride of thy Heart. What dost thou know of God? How little a portion is it? How #emph[immense] is he in his Nature? Canst thou look without terror into the #emph[Abyss] of Eternity? Thou canst not bear the rays of his #emph[glorious Being.]

Because I look on this #emph[Consideration] of great use in our #emph[walking] with God, so far as it #emph[may have] (as it may have) a Consistency with that #emph[filial Boldness] which is given us in Jesus Christ to #emph[draw nigh to the Throne of Grace,] I shall farther insist upon it, to give an abiding Impression of it to the Souls of them who desire to #emph[walk humbly with God.]

Consider then I say, to keep thy #emph[heart] in continual awe of the #emph[Majesty] of God, That #emph[Persons of the most high and eminent Attainments, of the nearest and most familiar Communion with God, do yet in this life know but a very little of him, and his Glory.] God reveals his Name to #emph[Moses,] the most glorious #emph[Attributes] that he hath manifested in the #emph[Covenant] of Grace, Exod 34:5, 6 yet all are but the #emph[back-parts] of God. All that he knows by it, is but little, low, compared to the #emph[perfection] of his Glory.

Hence it is with peculiar reference to #emph[Moses,] that it is said, #emph[No man hath seen God at any time,] John 1:18. Of him in #emph[comparison] with Christ doth he speak, v. 17 and of him it is here said, #emph[No man] (no not #emph[Moses,] the most eminent among them) #emph[hath seen God at any time.] We speak much of God, can talk of Him, his #emph[Ways,] his #emph[Works,] his #emph[Counsels] all the day long; The truth is, we know very #emph[little of him]\; our Thoughts, our Meditations, our Expressions of him are #emph[low,] many of them #emph[unworthy] of his #emph[Glory,] none of them reaching his Perfections.

You will say, that #emph[Moses] was under the #emph[Law,] when God #emph[wrapped] up himself in #emph[darkness,] and his Mind in #emph[Types] and #emph[Clouds] and dark Institutions. Under the glorious shining of the #emph[Gospel,] which hath brought #emph[Life] and #emph[Immortality] to #emph[light,] God being #emph[revealed] from his #emph[own bosom,] we now #emph[know] him much more clearly, and #emph[as he is:] We see his #emph[face] now, and not his #emph[back-parts only] as #emph[Moses] did.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Ans.] 1. I acknowledge a #emph[vast,] and almost #emph[unconceivable] difference between the #emph[Acquaintance] we now have with God,#footnote[Heb 1:2.] after his #emph[speaking] to us by his #emph[own Son,] and that which the generality of the #emph[Saints] had under the #emph[Law]: For although their #emph[eyes] were as good, #emph[sharp] and #emph[clear] as ours, their #emph[Faith] and spiritual #emph[understanding] not behind ours, the #emph[Object] as glorious unto them as unto us, yet our day is more clear than theirs was; the #emph[Clouds] are #emph[blown] away and scattered,#footnote[Song 4:6.] the #emph[shadows] of the #emph[Night] are gone and fled away, the #emph[Sun] is #emph[risen,] and the Means of sight is made more eminent and clear than formerly. Yet,]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. That peculiar sight which #emph[Moses] had of God, Exod 34 was a #emph[Gospel-sight,] a sight of God as #emph[Gracious, etc.] and yet it's called but his #emph[back-parts,] that is, but #emph[low] and #emph[mean,] in comparison of his #emph[Excellencies] and Perfections.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The Apostle #emph[exalting] to the #emph[utmost] this Glory of #emph[Gospel] Light above that of the #emph[Law,] manifesting that now the #emph[veil causing Darkness] is taken away;#footnote[ἀνακεκαλυμμένῳ προσώπῳ.] so that with open or #emph[uncovered face] we behold the #emph[Glory of the Lord]\; Tells us how: #emph[As in a glass,] 2 Cor 3:18. #emph[In a glass.] How is that? #emph[Clearly, perfectly?] Alas no: He tells you #emph[how] that is, 1 Cor 13:12. #emph[We see through a glass darkly,] saith he: It is not a #emph[Telescope,] that helps us to see things afar off, concerning which the Apostle speaks; and yet what poor helps are they! How short do we come of the Truth of things, notwithstanding their Assistance! It is a #emph[Looking-glass] whereunto he alludes, (where are only obscure #emph[species] and #emph[images] of things, and not the things themselves) and a sight therein, that he compares our Knowledge to: He tells you also that all that we do see δι’ ἐσόπτρου, #emph[by] or #emph[through] this #emph[Glass,] is in αἰνίγματι, in a #emph[Riddle,] in #emph[darkness] and Obscurity; And speaking of himself (who surely was much more #emph[clear sighted] than any now living) he tells us that he saw but ἐκ μέρους, #emph[in part]\; he saw but the #emph[back-parts] of heavenly things, v. 12 and compares all the #emph[knowledge] he had attained of God, to that he had of things when he was a #emph[child,] v. 11 it is a μέρος short of the τὸ τέλειον: yea such as καταργηθήσεται, it #emph[shall be destroyed] or done away. We know what #emph[weak, feeble,] uncertain #emph[Notions] and Apprehensions #emph[Children] have of #emph[things] of any abstruse Consideration; how when they #emph[grow] up with any Improvements of Parts and Abilities, those Conceptions vanish, and they are ashamed of them. It is the #emph[commendation] of a #emph[Child,] to love, honor, #emph[believe] and obey his Father; but for his #emph[Science] and Notions, his Father knows their #emph[childishness] and Folly. Notwithstanding all our Confidence of high Attainments, all our Notions of God are but #emph[childish] in respect of his infinite Perfections. We lisp and #emph[babble,] and say we know not what, for the most part; in our most #emph[accurate] (as we think) #emph[Conceptions] and Notions of God. We may #emph[love, honor, believe] and #emph[obey] our #emph[Father,] and therewith he accepts our Childish thoughts, for they are but Childish. We see but his #emph[back-parts,] we know but little of him. Hence is that Promise, wherewith we are so often supported and comforted in our Distress; #emph[we shall see him as he is]\; we shall #emph[see him face to face; know as we are known; comprehend that for which we are comprehended,] 1 Cor 13:12. 1 John 3:2 and positively, #emph[Now we see him not]: All concluding that here we see but his #emph[back-parts,] not as he is, but in a dark obscure #emph[representation,] not in the #emph[perfection] of his Glory.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The #emph[Queen] of #emph[Sheba] had heard much of #emph[Solomon,] and framed many great thoughts of his #emph[Magnificence] in her Mind thereupon; but when she came and saw his Glory, she was forced to confess, that #emph[the one half of the truth had not been told her.] We may suppose that we have here attained great knowledge, clear and high thoughts of God; but alas! when he shall bring us into his presence, we shall cry out, we never knew him as he is: The thousandth part of his Glory and Perfection and Blessedness, never entered into our hearts.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The Apostle tells us; 1 John 3:2. #emph[That we know not what we ourselves shall be]\; what we shall #emph[find] ourselves in the #emph[Issue]\; much less will it enter into our Hearts to conceive, what #emph[God is,] and what we shall #emph[find] him to be. Consider either #emph[him] who is to be #emph[known,] or the #emph[Way] whereby we know him, and this will farther appear.]]

==== We Know So Little of God

First, We know so little of #emph[God,] because it is #emph[God] who is thus to be known; that is, he who hath #emph[described] himself to us very much by this, #emph[That we cannot know him]: What else doth he intend where he calls himself #emph[invisible, incomprehensible,] and the like? That is, He whom we #emph[do not, cannot] know as he is; and our farther progress consists more in knowing what he #emph[is not,] than what he is. Thus is he described to be #emph[immortal, infinite]\; that is, he is not as #emph[we are, mortal, finite,] and #emph[limited.] Hence is that glorious description of him, 1 Tim 6:16. #emph[Who only hath Immortality, dwelling in the light which no man can approach unto, whom no man hath seen nor can see.] His #emph[light] is such as no Creature can #emph[approach unto]: He is not seen, not because he cannot be seen, but because we cannot bear the sight of him. The Light of God (#emph[in whom is no Darkness]) forbids all #emph[access] to him by any Creature whatever: We who cannot behold the Sun in its glory, are too too weak to bear the beams of Infinite Brightness.

On this Consideration (as was said) the wise man professeth himself #emph[a very Beast, and not to have the understanding of a Man,] Prov 30:2 that is, he knew nothing in comparison of God, so that he seemed to have lost all his Understanding, when once he came to the Consideration of him, his work and his ways. In this Consideration let our Souls descend to some #emph[particulars.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. For the #emph[Being] of God; we are so far from a knowledge of #emph[it,] so as to be able to #emph[instruct] one another therein, by words and #emph[expressions] of it, as that to frame any #emph[conceptions] in our Mind, with such #emph[species] and #emph[impressions] of things as we receive the knowledge of all other things by, is to make an #emph[Idol] to ourselves, and so to worship a God of our own #emph[making,] and not the #emph[God] that made us. We may as well and as #emph[lawfully] hew him out of #emph[wood,] or #emph[stone,] as form him a #emph[being] in our Minds suited to our Apprehensions.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The utmost of the best of our #emph[thoughts] of the being of God is, that we can have no #emph[thoughts] of it. Our knowledge of a #emph[being] is but #emph[low,] when it #emph[mounts] no #emph[higher] but only to know that we know it not.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. There be #emph[some] things of God, which he himself hath taught us to speak of, and to regulate our Expressions of them; but when we have so done, we see not the things themselves, we know them not: To #emph[believe] and #emph[admire] is all that we attain to. We profess (as we are taught,) that God is #emph[infinite, omnipotent, eternal]\; and we know what #emph[Disputes] and #emph[Notions] there are about Omnipresence, Immensity, Infiniteness and Eternity. We have (I say) #emph[words] and #emph[notions] about these things, but as to the things themselves, what do we know? What do we comprehend of them? Can the Mind of Man do any more but #emph[swallow] itself up in an #emph[infinite Abyss,] which is as nothing; give itself up to what it cannot #emph[conceive,] much less #emph[express?] Is not our #emph[understanding brutish] in the Contemplation of such things? And is as if it were not; yea the perfection of our #emph[understanding,] is, not to understand, and to rest there: They are but the #emph[back parts] of Eternity and #emph[Infiniteness] that we have a #emph[glimpse] of. What shall I say of the #emph[Trinity,] or the Subsistence of #emph[distinct Persons] in the same #emph[individual] Essence; a Mystery by many denied, because by none understood; a Mystery whose every letter is #emph[mysterious.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Who can declare the Generation of the #emph[Son,] the #emph[procession] of the #emph[Spirit,] or the #emph[difference] of the one from the other? But I shall not farther instance in particulars. That infinite and inconceivable #emph[distance] that is between #emph[Him] and us, keeps us in the dark as to any #emph[sight of his face,] or clear Apprehension of his Perfections.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[We know him rather by what he #emph[does,] than by what he is: by his #emph[doing] us #emph[good,] than by his #emph[essential] Goodness; and how little a portion of him (as #emph[Job] speaks) is hereby discovered?]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, We know little of God, because it is #emph[Faith] alone whereby here we know him; I shall not now discourse about the remaining Impressions on the Hearts of all men by Nature that there is a God, nor what they may Rationally be taught concerning that God, from the #emph[works] of his Creation and #emph[Providence,] which they see and behold; it is confessedly (and that upon the woeful experience of all Ages) so weak, low, dark, confused, that none ever on that #emph[account] glorified God as they ought, but notwithstanding all their #emph[knowledge] of God, were indeed #emph[without] God in the world.]

The chief and (upon the matter) almost only #emph[acquaintance] we have with God, and his #emph[Dispensations] of himself, is by #emph[Faith. He that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a Rewarder of them that seek him,] Heb 11:6. Our Knowledge of him, and his Rewarding, (the bottom of our Obedience or #emph[coming to him]) is #emph[believing. We walk by Faith, and not by Sight,] 2 Cor 5:7 διὰ πίστεως οὐ δι’ εἴδους; #emph[by Faith,] and so by #emph[Faith,] as not to have any express #emph[Idea, Image,] or #emph[species] of that which we believe: Faith is all the Argument we have of things not #emph[seen,] Heb 11:1. I might here insist upon the #emph[Nature] of it, and from all its #emph[Concomitants] and #emph[Concernments] manifest, that we know but the #emph[back-parts] of what we know by Faith only. As to its Rise, it is built purely upon the Testimony of him whom we have not #emph[seen]\; as the Apostle speaks, #emph[How can ye love him whom you have not seen?] That is, whom you know not but by Faith that he is: Faith receives all upon his #emph[Testimony,] whom it receives to #emph[be,] only upon his own Testimony. As to its Nature it is an #emph[Assent] upon Testimony, not an #emph[Evidence] upon Demonstration; and the #emph[Object] of it is (as was said before) above us. Hence our Faith (as was formerly observed) is called a #emph[seeing darkly as in a Glass:] All that we know this way (and all that we know of God we know this way) is but #emph[low,] and dark, and obscure.

But you will say, all this is true, but yet it is only so to them that know not God (perhaps) as he is revealed in Jesus Christ; with them who do so, 'tis otherwise. It is true, #emph[No man hath seen God at any time, but the only begotten Son, he hath revealed him,] John 1:17, 18 and, #emph[the Son of God is now come, and hath given us an understanding that we may know him that is true,] 1 John 5:20.

The Illumination of the glorious Gospel of Christ who is the #emph[Image of God shineth] upon Believers, 2 Cor 4:4 yea and #emph[God who commanded light to shine out of darkness, shines into their hearts, to give them the knowledge of his Glory in the face of his Son,] v. 6. So that though we were #emph[darkness,] yet we are now #emph[light in the Lord,] Eph 5:8. And the Apostle says, #emph[We all with open face behold the Glory of the Lord,] 2 Cor 3:18 and we are #emph[now] so far from being in such darkness, or at such a distance from God, that our #emph[communion and fellowship is with the Father and the Son,] 1 John 1:3. The Light of the Gospel whereby now God is revealed, is #emph[glorious]\; not a Star, but the #emph[Sun] in his beauty is risen upon us; and the #emph[veil] is taken from #emph[our faces]\; so that though #emph[unbelievers,] yea and perhaps some #emph[weak] Believers may be in some #emph[darkness,] yet those of any #emph[growth,] or considerable #emph[Attainments] have a clear sight and view of the #emph[face of God] in Jesus Christ.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[A.] 1. The truth is, we all of us #emph[know] enough of him to love him more than we do, to delight in him, and serve him, believe him, #emph[obey] him, put our trust in him above all that we have hitherto attained.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Our Darkness and Weakness is no Plea for our Negligence and Disobedience. Who is it that hath walked up to the Knowledge that he hath had of the #emph[Perfections,] Excellencies, and #emph[Will] of God? Gods End in #emph[giving] us any Knowledge of himself here, is that we may #emph[glorify him as God]\; that is, #emph[love] him, #emph[serve] him, #emph[believe] and #emph[obey] him, give him all the #emph[honor] and Glory that is due from poor sinful #emph[Creatures,] to a #emph[sin-pardoning] God, and Creator; we must all acknowledge, that we were never thoroughly transformed into the #emph[Image] of that Knowledge which we have had. And had we used our #emph[Talents] well, we might have been trusted with #emph[more.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Comparatively,] that knowledge which we have of God by the #emph[Revelation] of Jesus Christ in the #emph[Gospel,] is exceeding eminent and glorious. It is so in #emph[comparison] of any #emph[knowledge] of God that might otherwise be #emph[attained,] or was delivered in the #emph[Law] under the Old Testament, which had but the #emph[shadow of good things, not the express Image of them.] This the Apostle pursues at large, 2 Cor 3. Christ hath now in these last days revealed the #emph[Father from his own bosom, declared his Name,] made #emph[known] his Mind, Will and Counsel in a far more clear, eminent, distinct manner than he did formerly, whilst he kept his People under the #emph[pedagogy] of the #emph[Law]: And this is that which for the #emph[most part] is intended in the places before mentioned; the #emph[clear, perspicuous delivery] and declaration of God, and his Will in the Gospel is expressly exalted in comparison of any #emph[other] way of Revelation of himself.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The difference between #emph[Believers] and #emph[Unbelievers] as to Knowledge, is not so much in the Matter of their Knowledge, as in the Manner of #emph[knowing.] Unbelievers some of them may know more, and be able to say more of God, his Perfections and his #emph[will,] than many #emph[Believers,] but they know nothing as they ought: nothing in a right #emph[manner,] nothing #emph[spiritually] and #emph[savingly]\; nothing with an #emph[holy, heavenly light.] The excellency of a Believer is not, that he hath a #emph[large Apprehension] of things, but that what he doth #emph[apprehend] (which perhaps may be very little) He sees it in the #emph[light] of the Spirit of God, in a saving #emph[soul-transforming light:] And this is that which gives us #emph[communion] with God, and not #emph[prying] Thoughts, or #emph[curious] raised Notions.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Jesus Christ by his #emph[Word] and #emph[Spirit,] reveals to the Hearts of all his, God as a #emph[Father,] as a God in #emph[Covenant,] as a Rewarder, every way sufficiently to teach us to obey him here, and to lead us to his Bosom, to lie down there in the Fruition of him to Eternity: But yet now,]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. Notwithstanding all this, it is but a #emph[little portion] we know of him, we see but his #emph[back-parts.] For,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(1) The intendment of all Gospel #emph[Revelation] is not to #emph[unveil] Gods #emph[Essential Glory,] that we should see him as he is, but merely to declare so much of him as he #emph[knows sufficient] to be a bottom of our #emph[Faith, Love, Obedience,] and #emph[coming] to him. That is, of the #emph[Faith] which here he expects from us: Such services as beseem poor Creatures in the midst of Temptations; but when he calls us to #emph[eternal Admiration] and Contemplation, without Interruption, he will make a new manner of Discovery of himself, and the whole #emph[shape] of things, as it now lies before us, will depart as a shadow.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(2) We are #emph[dull] and #emph[slow of heart] to receive the things that are in the Word revealed. God by our Infirmity and weakness, keeping us in continual dependence on him, for Teachings and Revelations of himself out of his Word, never in this world bringing any Soul to the utmost of what is from the Word to be made out and discovered; so that although the way of Revelation in the Gospel be clear and evident, yet we know little of the things themselves that are revealed.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Let us then revive the use and intendment of this Consideration; will not a due Apprehension of this #emph[unconceivable] Greatness of God, and that infinite distance wherein we stand from him, fill the Soul with an holy and awful Fear of him; so as to keep it in a Frame unsuited to the thriving or #emph[flourishing] of any Lust whatever? Let the Soul be continually wonted to Reverential thoughts of Gods greatness and #emph[omnipresence,] and it will be much upon its #emph[watch,] as to any undue deportments; consider #emph[him] with whom you have to do; even #emph[our God is a consuming fire]\; and in your greatest Abashments at his #emph[presence] and #emph[eye,] know, that your very Nature is too narrow to bear #emph[apprehensions] suitable to his essential Glory.]]
